#! mathcas 2 notebook

#% title
# divisors

#% text
# `divisors(n)` gives the positive divisors of the integer `n` in increasing order.

#% section
# Details

#% item
# The sign of `n` is dropped. Large `n` are factored first, so the divisors of numbers with large prime factors are found quickly.

#% section
# Examples

#% subsection
# Basic examples

#% cell
divisors(12)
#% output
#= [1, 2, 3, 4, 6, 12]

#% cell
divisors(17)
#% output
#= [1, 17]

#% cell
divisors(-30)
#% output
#= [1, 2, 3, 5, 6, 10, 15, 30]

#% cell
divisors(1)
#% output
#= [1]

#% subsection
# Scope

#% text
# A large integer:

#% cell
divisors(2^10*3^3)
#% output
#= [1, 2, 3, 4, 6, 8, 9, 12, 16, 18, 24, 27, 32, 36, 48, 54, 64, 72, 96, 108, 128, 144, 192, 216, 256, 288, 384, 432, 512, 576, 768, 864, 1024, 1152, 1536, 1728, 2304, 3072, 3456, 4608, 6912, 9216, 13824, 27648]

#% text
# Each element of a list, with `map`:

#% cell
map(divisors, [6, 7, 8])
#% output
#= [[1, 2, 3, 6], [1, 7], [1, 2, 4, 8]]

#% subsection
# Applications

#% text
# The perfect numbers below 10000, equal to the sum of their proper divisors:

#% cell
select(range(2, 10000), n => sum(divisors(n)) == 2*n)
#% output
#= [6, 28, 496, 8128]

#% subsection
# Properties and relations

#% text
# The number of divisors is `divisor_sigma(0, n)`:

#% cell
[length(divisors(720)), divisor_sigma(0, 720)]
#% output
#= [30, 30]

#% section
# See also

#% text
# `divisor_sigma`, `divisor_sum`, `factor_integer`, `euler_phi`
